---
label: One list
---

One list whose rows the server can reorder. `Admin::TextTransformActions` is
the smallest complete implementation in the codebase; the samples below
follow it.

<%= embed Patterns::SortableListsPreview, :single_list, panels: %i[source] %>

The preview's move URL points at a path that does not exist, so a drop moves
the row, the request fails, and the row rolls back with an error toast. That
is the whole failure path; persistence needs the endpoint described at the
end of this tab.

## The root

One wrapper per page region. It carries the move URL template and the outlet
selectors, scoped to its own id:

```html
<div id="text_transform_actions_list"
     data-controller="sortable-lists"
     data-sortable-lists-move-url-template-value="<%%= move_url_template %>"
     data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--list-outlet="#text_transform_actions_list [data-controller~='sortable-lists--list']"
     data-sortable-lists-sortable-lists--item-outlet="#text_transform_actions_list [data-controller~='sortable-lists--item']">
```

Build the template from the route helper through a sentinel, so an
installation under a relative URL root keeps working; `{id}` is expanded on
the client with the dragged row's id:

```ruby
def move_url_template
  id_placeholder = "__id__"
  drop_admin_text_transform_action_path(id_placeholder).sub(id_placeholder, "{id}")
end
```

The expanded URL must stay same-origin and relative; only path, query and
fragment survive.

## The list

The list element declares the type it persists and the type it accepts.
`type` doubles as the `list_type` the server receives, so a list without one
cannot accept drops and warns in the console. `name` is what screen readers
hear when a row arrives from another list; give it even for one list, it
costs nothing and the list may gain siblings.

```html
<div data-controller="sortable-lists--list"
     data-sortable-lists--list-type-value="text_transform_action"
     data-sortable-lists--list-accepted-type-value="text_transform_action"
     data-sortable-lists--list-name-value="<%%= t(".title") %>">
  <ul>
    …rows…
  </ul>
</div>
```

Rows may sit in a child `<ul>`, as `BorderBoxList` renders them, or directly
under the list element. Both resolve without configuration, so table rows
and cards need no special markup.

## The row

Each row declares its identity and hosts the drag handle:

```html
<li data-controller="sortable-lists--item"
    data-sortable-lists--item-id-value="<%%= action.id %>"
    data-sortable-lists--item-type-value="text_transform_action"
    data-sortable-lists--item-label-value="<%%= action.label %>">
  <button type="button" class="handle" data-sortable-lists--item-target="handle" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">
    <%%= primer_octicon(:grabber) %>
  </button>
  …
</li>
```

`Primer::OpenProject::DragHandle` renders that button for you; give it the
`handle` target.

Without a `handle` target the whole row is the handle, which fights with
links and buttons inside it. Add a `preview` target on an inner element when
the row's own box is not what should follow the pointer (a card inside a
padded `<li>`, for instance); the default preview is the row itself.

## The move menu

A drag is a pointer gesture, so every sortable row also needs a keyboard-
and screen-reader-reachable path. Put move actions in the row's Primer
`ActionMenu`; the controller depends on that component. Each action is a
`moveItem` target with a `direction` param (`top`, `up`, `down`, `bottom`)
and the `move` action, passed to `with_item` so the attributes land on the
menu's `<li>`, not on the button inside it:

```erb
<%%= render(Primer::Alpha::ActionMenu.new) do |menu| %>
  <%% menu.with_show_button(icon: "kebab-horizontal", scheme: :invisible, "aria-label": action.label) %>
  <%% menu.with_divider(data: { sortable_lists__item_target: "moveDivider" }) %>
  <%% menu.with_item(
        label: "Move up",
        tag: :button,
        data: {
          sortable_lists__item_target: "moveItem",
          sortable_lists__item_direction_param: "up",
          action: "click->sortable-lists--item#move"
        }
      ) %>
<%% end %>
```

`move` looks up the row's `<action-menu>` element and asks it whether that
`<li>` is disabled or hidden before moving anything; the click reaches the
`<li>` by bubbling up from the button. A row without an `ActionMenu` still
drags, its `move` action just does nothing. The `moveDivider` target on the
divider above the group lets the controller hide it together with the
actions when none applies.

Availability is recomputed every time the menu opens: the first row has no
"up", the last no "down". Unavailable actions are hidden; set
`data-sortable-lists--item-hide-unavailable-value="false"` on the row to
disable them instead.

## The backend request

A drop, or a move action, sends `PUT` to the expanded move URL with
`responseKind: 'turbo-stream'` and three form fields:

| Field | Value for one list |
| --- | --- |
| `list_type` | the list's `type` |
| `list_id` | blank; the list declares no `id` |
| `prev_id` | id of the row the moved row now follows, or blank when it landed first |

`prev_id` is an anchor, not an index: a stale ordinal cannot silently move
the wrong row. A drop that leaves the row where it was sends nothing.

The row moves in the DOM before the request resolves. The endpoint answers
by streaming the re-rendered list, and the morph replaces the optimistic
placement with the server's order. A failure rolls the row back; a `422`
stays silent because the server has already streamed its own message, any
other status raises a generic toast.

For `acts_as_list` models the endpoint is a few lines with the
`Lists::MoveAfterAnchor` concern; the Reference tab describes the server
half, including the anchor rules and what to do when the model orders by
something other than a position column.
